<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in WAT to EST Conversions</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>1. Expecting Daylight Saving Time Shifts in West Africa</h3>
    <p>
      Countries observing West Africa Time do not alter their clocks seasonally. WAT remains fixed at UTC+1 year-round. Never adjust WAT for summer daylight saving.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>2. Conflating EST with EDT</h3>
    <p>
      EST is UTC-5 (winter), while EDT is UTC-4 (summer). The offset between Lagos and New York is 6 hours in winter, but narrows to 5 hours in summer.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>3. Mixing WAT with Central Africa Time (CAT) or East Africa Time (EAT)</h3>
    <p>
      WAT is UTC+1. CAT is UTC+2 (e.g., Harare, Lusaka) and EAT is UTC+3 (e.g., Nairobi). Conflating these acronyms causes 1-to-2 hour scheduling errors.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>4. Date Boundary Rollover Errors</h3>
    <p>
      Converting early morning WAT timestamps (e.g., 03:00 AM WAT) yields 9:00 PM EST on the previous calendar evening. Always verify date markers.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- TECHNICAL TIMEZONE DATABASE SECTION -->
<section class="content" id="technical-details" aria-labelledby="tech-heading">
  <h2 id="tech-heading">Technical Specifications & IANA Timezone Database Metadata</h2>
  <p>
    Software developers require exact database keys to perform automated timezone calculations across enterprise systems.
  </p>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Canonical Identifiers</h3>
      <p>The primary IANA time zone key for West Africa is <code>Africa/Lagos</code>. Alternative aliases include <code>Africa/Luanda</code>, <code>Africa/Kinshasa</code>, <code>Africa/Douala</code>, and <code>Africa/Algiers</code>.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>ISO 8601 Offset Representation</h3>
      <p>WAT is expressed in ISO 8601 string formats as <code>+01:00</code> (e.g., <code>2026-07-30T15:00:00+01:00</code>).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>POSIX Timezone Specification</h3>
      <p>The standard POSIX string for West Africa Time is <code>WAT-1</code>, denoting standard time 1 hour ahead of UTC without DST shifts.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Banking & Financial Network Sync</h3>
      <p>Financial transaction networks managed by NIBSS (Nigeria Inter-Bank Settlement System) synchronize timestamps against UTC/WAT atomic time standards.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

